I've read both files. Let me analyze the key constraints before writing.

**The core problem:** Note 7 says the geospatial camera clamps pitch against the radius it is applied with — from `4*planetRadius` pitch is forced to `0.001`, falling linearly from `2R`. Note 45 confirms: at the start radius (`4*planetRadius`) a pitch set there stays ~0. So naively calling `setGeospatialOrientation({ pitch })` at the default zoomed-out radius would silently clamp my pitch to ~0.

**The fix:** Note 37 says `limits.pitchDisabledRadiusScale = null` allows full pitch at any radius. This is the clean lever — it disables the radius-based pitch clamp entirely, so my pitch survives at any zoom, and it persists because the limits object is stored on the camera.

**Ordering:** Note 7 says "Set radius first, or both in `setGeospatialOrientation`." Since I'm passing radius explicitly (unchanged) alongside pitch in one `setGeospatialOrientation` call, the clamp is evaluated against the correct radius. And note 37 says a limits change applies at the next yaw/pitch/radius write — so I set `pitchDisabledRadiusScale = null` *before* the orientation call so the very write that sets pitch sees the disabled scale.

**What must not change:** I read `camera.radius`, `camera.yaw`, `camera.center` and pass them back unchanged, so zoom, heading, and centre are preserved. Because I don't assign a new `center` object (note 11), no re-orbit happens.

One subtlety worth flagging: `pitchDisabledRadiusScale = null` also means user-driven pitch rotations via `attachGeospatialControls` will no longer be limited by zoom (note 67 clamps to `[0, PI/2-0.001]` regardless, so that's unaffected — but the *radius-dependent* softening is gone). For a globe viewer that wants a stable tilt at every zoom, that's the intended trade-off.

FILE: app/src/globe-tilt.ts
```ts
import type { GeospatialCamera } from "@babylonjs/lite";
import { setGeospatialOrientation } from "@babylonjs/lite";

/**
 * Tilts the geospatial camera so it looks at its centre point at the given
 * pitch, without changing the zoom (radius), heading (yaw) or centre point.
 *
 * @param camera The geospatial camera to adjust.
 * @param pitch Pitch in radians, measured from looking straight down at the
 *              ground (0) toward the horizon. Expected in roughly [0.1, 1.2].
 */
export function tiltGlobeCamera(camera: GeospatialCamera, pitch: number): void {
    // Disable the radius-dependent pitch clamp so the requested pitch holds at
    // any zoom level, including the fully-zoomed-out starting radius where the
    // default limits would otherwise force pitch back to ~0.
    camera.limits.pitchDisabledRadiusScale = null;

    // Re-apply the orientation with the same radius, yaw and centre so only the
    // pitch changes. Passing radius alongside pitch ensures the clamp (now
    // disabled) is evaluated against the correct radius.
    setGeospatialOrientation(camera, {
        yaw: camera.yaw,
        pitch: pitch,
        radius: camera.radius,
        center: camera.center,
    });
}
```

I wrote `src/globe-tilt.ts` to disable the radius-based pitch clamp (`limits.pitchDisabledRadiusScale = null`) and then re-apply the orientation via `setGeospatialOrientation` with the unchanged radius, yaw, and centre, so only the pitch changes and it holds at every zoom. I'm not fully certain whether `setGeospatialOrientation` is exported from the package root `"@babylonjs/lite"` versus a sub-path — the `.d.ts` declares it in `geospatial-camera.d.ts`, so if your import map routes that module differently, adjust the import path accordingly.
